Bioshock is a brilliantly written with amazing gameplay, graphics and storyline. The true essence of Bioshock is captured by the beautiful and down right amazing storyline of the fallen Atlantis of a city, Rapture. The graphics are very good for something made in 2007 and very light weight on PC's everywhere. What I love the most is the games captivating take on Ayn Rand's Philosophy of objectivism and how that very philosophy would be the very fall of Rapture as it is.


The game takes place in an underwater city known as Rapture, it was forged by the dreams of a wealthy Russian man by the name of Andrew Ryan. (or in Russian
) His Dream was to escape the clutching hands of political, religious, and social anxieties of a somewhat post WWII world. Rapture was built November 5th, 1946 and established in 1951. Ryan sought out to make a so called "perfect world" filled with men and women of free will and not men and women of the "parasites". Fast forward to December 31st, 1958 when the 1958 New Years Eve riots were started by no other then the man who was your so called friend in the game, Atlas. This caused major panic and many died in this act of terrorism. By 1960 the man who was posing as Atlas, Frank Fontaine was feeling the pressure of Ryan's pheromone-controlled army what what is called Splicers. In a last effort to turn things to his liking Fontaine called for the help of Andrew Ryan's illegitimate son Jack who was brainwashed in the the effects of a sleeper agent, someone who is activated by a simple word or phrase to complete a task preset by the activator. And we all know what that is "Would you Kindly". And then begins the events of Bioshock 1 where you play as Jack who is the sole survivor that suffers from a plane crash and has no other choice but to go into the creepy lighthouse the he ironically crashed in front of.

There's one spoiler in this review and it is marked as such. You are warned.

